i can't help myself i like good food  okay and  good food is hard for a rat to find  thanks to tick-tock ratatouille has  officially reached  new heights as the beloved cultural  phenomenon it always deserves to be  but why is that and why now sure the  animation is amazing  how did you do that the story is  heartwarming the characters are lovable  and the food looks delicious but why did  the message of ratatouille hit so hard  at this exact moment  in time keep watching to find out anyone  can cook well yeah anyone can  that doesn't mean that anyone should  all the way back in 2007 when we first  heard that pixar was making  a movie about a rat cooking food in a  restaurant kitchen we thought  gross i saw it having a rat anywhere  near food just seems like  a bad idea and to make a whole animated  movie about it  it'll never work and yet just like ego  the snooty food critic with his old  school beliefs about what defines a real  chef  by the end of the movie we were totally  won over  let rats cook all of the meals okay  so maybe not in real life but in this  case the story of remy and his human  friend linguini  who arise from the bottom to be declared  the finest chefs in all of france  is without a doubt one of the best pixar  stories of all time  so i'm not crazy sure it might not have  been as big of a sensation as toy story  as action-packed as the incredibles or  have as many iconic lines as monsters  inc  may the best monster win but we think  that ratatouille stands out from the  rest of the pixar lineup  for so many reasons to start with it has  all of the story elements that make up  an awesome pixar movie  a hero who we meet while he's  questioning the life he feels  inescapably trapped in  before he falls down to his lowest point  in this case literally in the sewer  and then we get to go along for the ride  as he climbs to his eventual triumph  over all  obstacles it has gorgeous animation a  beautiful score  some great jokes and a cast of equally  lovable supporting characters  but on top of all of that we get the  ending and the ending is what makes  ratatouille one of the best films that  pixar has ever made  because it's not just about a rat who  wants to be a chef  it's about what it really means to be an  artist to have an unshakable need to  create something beautiful  even when the world tries to stop you in  every possible way  you with me it's about class and the  barricades that have been put in place  to make sure that only a select few are  able to rise to the top  even the meal that remy cooks to finally  win over the formidable ego  ratatouille means so much more than it  might seem  it's a vegetable stew that was  originally made by poor farmers in the  southern part of france  and by making it for ego remy  immediately transports him back to his  own humble beginnings  and reminds him that good food means so  much more than how  fancy it is how difficult it was to  prepare or where the chef learned to  cook  sure almost all pixar movies have their  own heartwarming message at the end  but as we live through a time that is  challenged so much more about the world  we inhabit  it's no accident that this movie has  recently hit home with millions of  people  and why ratatouille has found its new  home on tick tock  we have to wonder would ratatouille have  had such a massive comeback if we  weren't currently in the middle of a  global pandemic  what's more important here some of us  stuck at home with not a whole lot to do  to keep busy  and would any of it have happened  without tick tock  see way back in august we were all still  figuring out what to do with the huge  amount of free time on our hands  in a world that had changed  significantly many turn to tick tock as  a way to entertain themselves  including one user named emily jacobson  who innocently uploaded a little clip of  herself singing a song about remy the  rat  with no idea what kind of storm she had  just set in motion  the quick video went viral which is when  composer daniel mertzlaft  got involved and really turned things up  a notch  he transformed the simple tune into a  triumphant orchestral finale  in which he envisioned glitter and  confetti reigning onto the audience as  40  ensemble singers triumphantly belted the  tune to their beloved remy  standing center stage it didn't take  long for the ball to continue to roll  as songs like ratatouille tango there's  good in the garbage  and anyone can cook appeared on the  platform rounding out the show and  giving a voice to all of the different  characters  and then it wasn't just singers and  composers who were getting involved  all of the choreographers set designers  costume designers stage managers and  lighting designers who might have been  too busy to participate in previous  years  hopped on to the ratatouille train and  brought the show's visuals to life  and just like that out of nowhere  thousands of creators with no theaters  to work in and millions of spectators  no shows to go and see were actually  creating a full-scale broadway musical  together  from scratch while sitting in their  living rooms alone  this wasn't just a funny meme anymore  ratatouille the musical became an  outlet for creators and audiences to  come together once again  after months of feeling isolated alone  and  completely cut off from what once gave  them so much joy  thanks for coming back little chef and  just like the ending of ratatouille  the movie they tore down the barriers  that normally stand between artists  and their ability to work there were no  auditions no casting directors no  gatekeepers deciding who would be  allowed in or not  because of what school they went to or  how long their resume was just like remy  and his cooking  this was all about the love of making  something wonderful for  everyone to enjoy ratatouille the  musical was more about the community  making it than the actual thing itself  and that is something that is pretty  rare these days  while it is possible that ratatouille  the movie might have found a new  audience all these years later on its  own  and without a global pandemic to kick  things off we're not so sure that  ratatouille the musical would have  and we don't think it's a coincidence  that this movie with its message that  art can come from anyone  no matter who or where they are  resonated so clearly with all of us  right now for millions the pandemic has  made us feel as though our entire lives  have been taken away from us  as though we had lost all of the things  that had once made us feel happy and  busy and fulfilled  whether you were a musician or an actor  with no stage to play on or an audience  member who could no longer get so much  joy out of going to see a show with your  friends and family  we all found ourselves feeling a little  bit lost  but coming together over tick-tock to  bond over the story of a little rat  named remy  really helped so many people feel a tiny  bit more  found  and of course everything we love about  ratatouille always brings us back to the  ending  when the movie came out in 2007 the  world was a very different place  and sure we thought that the message of  the movie was sweet but  it didn't really resonate in the same  way it does now let's take a quick look  at where the four most important  characters started off at the beginning  of the movie  we first meet remy when he and his  family are forced to hide in the walls  of a human's home  constantly fearing for their lives and  scrounging for food in order to survive  his family considers his dream of being  an artist frivolous because for them  mere survival is their only goal first  of all we  are not thieves secondly stay out of the  kitchen  linguine is a young man who has achieved  a position as a chef in a very important  restaurant  even though he doesn't really have all  that many skills has no trading and  is seriously clumsy we find out later  that his success  hasn't been earned but has been awarded  to him just because he's the son of the  great  august cousteau and heir to his legacy  join us we then have colette the only  woman working in the kitchen  who has had to work much harder than any  of her colleagues simply because she is  who she is  and who is understandably hesitant to  help linguini who has had everything she  dreams of  simply handed to him you are the boss  inspire him and finally we have ego the  man who has been declared the only voice  in deciding what is good  and what is bad and delights and  striking fear into the hearts of chefs  all over paris  he has long since upheld the old world  ways of french cuisine that  only favor those who are exactly like  the men who came before  with open disdain for anyone or anything  that is considered  different i don't like food  i love it until he tastes  remy's ratatouille that is as soon as  the so-called peasant food hits ego's  taste buds  he is immediately reminded that he too  was once on the outside  dreaming of a world that seemed like it  would never welcome him  he is brought back to what good food  really means  family love and acceptance and when he  is finally introduced to the real chef  behind his transformative meal  all of the rules and guidelines that he  has spent years enforcing about who is  allowed to cook  good food go right out the window he  admits that the food he ate  and more so the chef who cooked it  challenged his idea of what  fine cooking really is and as he said  rocked him to his core ego who once  openly mocked the great chef gusto's  motto  anyone can cook now understands that  that is actually  completely true but i realize only now  do i truly  understand what he meant but it doesn't  actually matter what you look like where  you come from or what kind of access you  were born into  for the first time he realized the most  fundamental part of that message  not everyone can become a great artist  but a great artist can come from  anywhere the ending of ratatouille makes  us wonder  just how much of what we believe to be  good or bad are  outdated rules that were taught to us  when we were younger  it asks us to question the way we judge  other people because of where they come  from  or what they look like or how much money  they have because  when it comes down to it none of these  things actually matter in the end  everyone deserves a fair chance to be  who they want to be and  create the things they want to create  even if they are just a tiny little rat  named remy with a dream of making  delicious food  and that dear friends is exactly why the  ending of ratatouille is the  best thing to happen to pixar we're not  crying or you're crying  well we all thought that we'd need tons  of amazon packages and elaborate  skincare routines to get us through the  pandemic  it turns out all we really needed was a  little ratatouille  what about you were you just as  enchanted as we were by this pixar  classic  or do you have another animated favorite  let us know in the comments and don't  forget to hit that subscribe button to  keep up with all the latest videos from  the things
 

